9 "q-351678" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530450 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530450 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}=(3x-3)+{2}^{4}(3x-3)^{2}+{3}^{4}(3x-3)^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530450 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48d3004f-21c4-5fdb-bf3a-f534994324c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530450 #edb/ref 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{4}(3x-3)^{n+1}}{{n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{4}(3x-3)}{{n}^{4}}| \\\\  & =|3x-3|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{4} \\\\  & =|3x-3|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x-1|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-1|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530462 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530462 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103164" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530462 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530462 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-6)^{n}$ converges at  $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nI. The series converges at $x=2$\n\nII. The series converges at $x=4$\n\nIII. The series diverges at $x=\\frac{7}{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530462 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ce7ead8-e0e1-5d3e-bcf5-334f7b5e90bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530462 #edb/ref 1074 "The center of the expansion is $x=6,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{7}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{7}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}).$\n\n![](images/f7/f7b2a20d097d3ff34f92ff7f0015c449150575420dd53132d0b2703567628ef5.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $2\\notin [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $4\\in [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),$ the series is convergent at $x=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\frac{7}{2}\\in [\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}),$ the series is convergent at $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530475 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530475 #edb/ref 1069 "q-354475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530475 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530475 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{n}^{3}(5x+10)^{n}=(5x+10)+{2}^{3}(5x+10)^{2}+{3}^{3}(5x+10)^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530475 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71001133-6021-56cc-9d7c-8178573baa4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530475 #edb/ref 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{3}(5x+10)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(5x+10)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}(5x+10)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(5x+10)}{{n}^{3}}| \\\\  & =|5x+10|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{3} \\\\  & =|5x+10|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5|x+2|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x+2|<\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530481 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}{n!}=(x+1)+\\frac{{2}^{2}(x+1)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{3}^{2}(x+1)^{3}}{3!}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ccf1362-83a8-5f48-9ecd-741f9bd50f82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530481 #edb/ref 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(x+1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(x+1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|x+1|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n+1}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =|x+1|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530482 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530482 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530482 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-4)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nI. The series diverges at $x=3$\n\nII. The series converges at $x=-2$\n\nIII. The series converges at $x=1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f3e3995-bd16-5e9f-8391-9d6b04505974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530482 #edb/ref 1074 "The center of the expansion is $x=4,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $5,$ i.e., $R\\ge 5.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-1,9).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-1,9).$\n\n![](images/c0/c0f5ebe0de173431758aed0680a6795267582aa2bc209702253d3d2828c91d02.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $3\\in [-1,9),$ the series is convergent at $x=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-2\\notin [-1,9),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $1\\in [-1,9),$ the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530497 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(3x-6)^{n}}{(n!)^{2}}=(3x-6)+\\frac{(3x-6)^{2}}{(2!)^{2}}+\\frac{(3x-6)^{3}}{(3!)^{2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b8b4204-5ca8-58b7-aeeb-092f50cd6610" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530497 #edb/ref 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(3x-6)^{n}}{(n!)^{2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(3x-6)^{n+1}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}}{\\frac{(3x-6)^{n}}{(n!)^{2}}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(3x-6)^{n+1}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n!)^{2}}{(3x-6)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|3x-6|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =|3x-6|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
